course: language
lesson: 37
title: Building words: prefixes, suffixes and compounds
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Long words are often built from smaller parts, like a train made of carriages: a root in the middle, a prefix hooked on the front and a suffix hooked on the back.
explain: Ruby's teacher writes "unhappiness" on the board; Ruby spots three carriages: "un-" (not), "happy" (the root, with its y turned to i) and "-ness" (which turns it into a naming word).
explain: English also glues whole words together, "book" + "keeper" = "bookkeeper", and blends bits of words, "hungry" + "angry" = "hangry", the grumpy feeling right before lunch.
explain: Sometimes people even build words backwards, chopping off what looks like an ending: "burglar" came first, and "burgle" was made from it later.
explain: Knowing the parts lets you crack new words like a code, but not every combination is a real word, so check new ones in a dictionary.
fact: A root is the main part of a word, a prefix is added to the start and a suffix to the end: "un" + "happy" + "ness" makes "unhappiness".
fact: Some prefixes and their meanings: re- (again), un- (not, or undo), dis- (not, or the opposite), pre- (before), mis- (wrongly).
fact: The suffix "-ful" means full of and "-less" means without: hopeful and hopeless, careful and careless; "-ful" is spelled with one l.
fact: A blend joins parts of two words: brunch (breakfast + lunch), smog (smoke + fog), motel (motor + hotel) and hangry (hungry + angry), which is now in major dictionaries.
fact: "Supercalifragilisticexpialidocious", from the 1964 film Mary Poppins, has 34 letters and means wonderful; it is a made-up word, and many of the longest dictionary words are technical ones.
fact: A compound word is two words joined into one; "bookkeeper" (book + keeper) has three double letters in a row: oo, kk, ee.
fact: Back-formation means making a new word by chopping off what looks like an ending: "burgle" was made from "burglar", and "edit" from "editor".
fact: "-ish" is an old English suffix meaning somewhat, as in childish and reddish; today people also say "7-ish" and even answer just "Ish."
fact: "Hamburger" comes from Hamburg, a city in Germany, not from ham; people split it into "ham" + "burger" and then made "cheeseburger" and "veggie burger".
fact: "Dead" as a booster meaning very ("dead good") is common in Liverpool and much of northern England; in Leicester and the East Midlands "me duck" is a friendly word, like love or dear.
fact: "Selfie" is "self" plus the friendly ending "-ie", a favourite in Australian English (brekkie for breakfast, barbie for barbecue); it was Oxford Dictionaries' Word of the Year in 2013.
exercise: Ruby splits "unhappiness". What are its three parts? | un, happi, ness | unh, app, iness | un, hap, piness | unha, pp, iness
exercise: What does the prefix "re-" mean in "redo"? | Again | Not | Before | Wrongly
exercise: Leo says he'll "unredo" his homework. Why does his teacher laugh? | Not every mix of parts makes a real word | "Un-" can never go before any other prefix | "Redo" is not a real English word | "Un-" means "again", so it means do it twice
exercise: What does the suffix "-less" mean in "careless"? | Without | Full of | A little bit | Again
exercise: Which spelling is correct? | Careful | Carefull | Carful | Caerful
exercise: Kai's sister says she's "hangry". What two words were blended? | Hungry and angry | Hanging and angry | Hand and grumpy | Happy and angry
exercise: Which word is a blend of breakfast and lunch? | Brunch | Bunch | Lunchfast | Breakch
exercise: How many letters are in "Supercalifragilisticexpialidocious"? | 34 | 28 | 43 | 24
exercise: Zara looks at "bookkeeper". Which three double letters come one after another? | oo, kk, ee | bb, oo, pp | oo, ee, rr | kk, ee, pp
exercise: Why does "bookkeeper" have so many double letters? | It is two words, book and keeper, joined | It is a misspelling that dictionaries accept | It comes from a Dutch word with double letters | The printer Caxton added letters to it
exercise: Harry's grandad says "burgle" came from "burglar". What is this called? | Back-formation | A blend | A compound word | A palindrome
exercise: Which word was made from "editor" by back-formation? | Edit | Edition | Editorial | Credit
exercise: What does "-ish" mean in "the party's at 7-ish"? | Roughly, somewhat | Exactly, on the dot | Late, after the time | Early, before the time
exercise: Where does the word "hamburger" come from? | Hamburg, a city in Germany | Ham, the meat inside it | A cook called Mr Hamburg | Hamlet, the play by Shakespeare
exercise: A Scouse lad says "That's dead good." What does "dead" mean here? | Very | Not alive | Boring | Old
exercise: Which friendly ending turned "self" into "selfie"? | -ie, popular in Australian English | -fie, from an old French ending | -lfie, a Latin ending for photos | -e, added by phone spellcheckers
test: Gran snaps "-ish isn't a word, it's lazy talk!" What's true? | "-ish" is an old English suffix meaning somewhat, now used more freely | She's right, "-ish" was made up on the internet last year by gamers | "-ish" is only allowed in maths lessons about time | "-ish" is an American spelling of "-ness"
test: Your mate goes "there's ham in a hamburger, obviously, it's in the name." What's the truth? | The name comes from Hamburg; the "ham" bit is not about meat | He's right, the very first hamburgers were made from sliced ham | It comes from "hammer", because the meat is flattened | It comes from Hamlet, who loved burgers
test: Your Leicester mate says "proper good, me duck" about your drawing. ("Me duck" means love or dear.) What does she think of it? | She thinks it's really good | She thinks it looks like a duck | She thinks it's a bit rubbish | She wants you to draw her a duck
test: What would happen if nobody had ever split "hamburger" into "ham" + "burger"? | We probably wouldn't say "cheeseburger" or "veggie burger" | We would have no word at all for sandwiches | Hamburgers would be called Hamburg pies by law | Nothing at all would change in English
test: Your little sis writes "hopefull" in her story. What do you tell her? | The suffix "-ful" has only one l | It needs a third l: "hopefulll" | It should be "hopeless" instead | Both l's are right, keep it
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "brunch and smog are just slang, they don't count." What's fairer? | They're blends, and many blends become normal words | He's right, blends are never real words in any dictionary | Only blends from Latin count as real words | Blends only count if a king approves them
test: Ollie reckons "Supercalifragilisticexpialidocious" is the longest real word in every dictionary. What's true? | It's a made-up film word; the longest dictionary words are often technical | He's right, it's officially the longest English word in every dictionary | It's a Greek medical word for a fever | It's the longest word in Welsh, not English
test: Your grandad from Liverpool says "burglar must come from burgle, lad, verbs come first." What's true? | "Burglar" came first and "burgle" was made from it | He's right, verbs always come before the nouns made from them | Both words appeared on the very same day | "Burgle" comes from "burger", not "burglar"
test: Your mate makes "misunderstand" and asks what "mis-" adds. What's the answer? | Wrongly: to understand wrongly | Again: to understand twice | Before: to understand early | Not at all: to never hear
test: Your cousin from Perth says "grab ya togs, we'll take a selfie at the beach." ("Togs" means swimming costume.) What is a selfie? | A photo you take of yourself | A swim on your own | A beach towel with your name | A type of Australian sunscreen
test: Which of these is a compound word? | Football | Unkind | Hopeful | Brunch
exam: Identify the constituent parts of the word "unhappiness". | A prefix, a root and a suffix | Two roots and no affixes | Three separate prefixes | A suffix, a prefix and a second suffix
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of the verb "burgle"? | It was derived from the pre-existing noun "burglar" by back-formation | The noun "burglar" was derived from the pre-existing verb "burgle" by suffixation | Both were borrowed simultaneously from Latin | It is a blend of "burger" and "gargle"
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the word "hamburger". | Its name derives from the German city of Hamburg rather than from ham | Its name derives from ham, its original principal ingredient | Its name derives from a cook surnamed Hamburg | Its name derives from Shakespeare's play Hamlet
exam: Ascertain the meaning conveyed by the suffix "-less" in the word "hopeless". | Without | Full of | Somewhat | Before
